Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• `comments.text`: body of the Hacker News comment – search for the string “stackoverflow.com/questions/” to detect a reference to a SO question.  
• `comments.time_ts` (or `comments.time`): timestamp of the comment – filter to on/after 2014‑01‑01.  
• `comments.id`: unique id of the comment – can be joined to the same id that appears in the denormalised table `full_partition_extra` in order to fetch any pre–computed metadata (e.g. tags).  
• `full_partition_extra.tags` (ARRAY<STRING>): list of tag strings that were harvested for that HN item; each element may itself contain one or more tags glued together with the “|” character, so we will have to split them.  
• `full_partition_extra.type`: make sure we are looking at comments (`type = 'comment'`).  
• `full_partition_extra.timestamp`: another timestamp field that can be used for date filtering.  

Typical filter conditions  
• `LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'` – the comment mentions a Stack Overflow question.  
• `(comments.time_ts >= '2014-01-01')` – we only keep comments from 2014 onward.  
• `ARRAY_LENGTH(tags) > 0` – keep only rows that actually contain tags.  

Below are ten exploratory BigQuery statements, gradually moving from simple inspection toward the final aggregation of the top‑10 tags.  
All queries use only the tables that are available inside the dataset
`fh-bigquery.hackernews`.  

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at raw comments that mention a Stack Overflow question
SELECT id,
       time_ts,
       text
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
WHERE  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Same as #1 but only for comments on/after 2014‑01‑01
SELECT id,
       time_ts,
       text
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
  AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Extract the numeric Question‑ID from the Stack Overflow URL
SELECT id                         AS comment_id,
       REGEXP_EXTRACT(LOWER(text),
                      r'stackoverflow\.com/questions/([0-9]+)') AS so_qid
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
  AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join the raw comments with the denormalised “extra” table
--    (to see whether tags are present for the same comment‑id)
SELECT c.id            AS comment_id,
       e.type,
       e.tags
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`            AS c
JOIN   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra` AS e
  ON   c.id = e.id
WHERE  c.time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
  AND  LOWER(c.text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Inspect any row in full_partition_extra that already contains tags
SELECT id,
       type,
       tags
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra`
WHERE  ARRAY_LENGTH(tags) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Flatten all tag strings in full_partition_extra
SELECT tag
FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra`,
       UNNEST(tags) AS tag
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Build a list of comment‑ids that mention SO questions (2014+)
WITH so_comments AS (
  SELECT id
  FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
  WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
    AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
)
SELECT sc.id,
       e.tags
FROM   so_comments                                AS sc
JOIN   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra` AS e
  ON   sc.id = e.id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. After isolating those comments, explode the tags
WITH so_comments AS (
  SELECT id
  FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
  WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
    AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
),
tag_list AS (
  SELECT e.id,
         tag_string
  FROM   so_comments                                AS sc
  JOIN   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra` AS e
    ON   sc.id = e.id
  CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.tags) AS tag_string
)
SELECT tag_string
FROM   tag_list
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Individual tag strings can still contain “|”-separated values.
--    Split them into truly atomic tags (lower‑cased).
WITH so_comments AS (
  SELECT id
  FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
  WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
    AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
),
tag_list AS (
  SELECT e.id,
         tag_string
  FROM   so_comments                                AS sc
  JOIN   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra` AS e
    ON   sc.id = e.id
  CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.tags) AS tag_string
)
SELECT LOWER(t) AS atomic_tag
FROM   tag_list,
       UNNEST(SPLIT(tag_list.tag_string, '|')) AS t
WHERE  t IS NOT NULL AND t <> ''
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final aggregation: top‑10 Stack Overflow tags most often
--     referenced via Hacker News comments (2014+)
WITH so_comments AS (
  SELECT id
  FROM   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.comments`
  WHERE  time_ts >= '2014-01-01'
    AND  LOWER(text) LIKE '%stackoverflow.com/questions%'
),
tag_list AS (
  SELECT e.id,
         tag_string
  FROM   so_comments                                AS sc
  JOIN   `fh-bigquery.hackernews.full_partition_extra` AS e
    ON   sc.id = e.id
  CROSS JOIN UNNEST(e.tags) AS tag_string
),
atomic_tags AS (
  SELECT LOWER(t) AS tag
  FROM   tag_list,
         UNNEST(SPLIT(tag_list.tag_string, '|')) AS t
  WHERE  t IS NOT NULL AND t <> ''
)
SELECT tag,
       COUNT(*) AS mention_cnt
FROM   atomic_tags
GROUP  BY tag
ORDER  BY mention_cnt DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

Each query is limited to a small result set for inspection (`LIMIT 20` for exploratory
queries, `LIMIT 10` for the final ranking).  
Together they illustrate a complete, step‑by‑step approach – from detecting Stack Overflow
links in Hacker News comments to breaking down and counting the individual tags.
